From: V4L2 WebRTC encode enablement
Subject: Enable kVaapiVideoEncodeLinux by default for V4L2 HW encode on Linux

Chromium gates hardware video encoding on Linux behind the
kVaapiVideoEncodeLinux feature flag (despite the name, it controls
V4L2 encoders too, not just VAAPI). This flag is checked in
render_thread_impl.cc to determine HW encoder availability.

Enable it by default so V4L2 hardware encoders (e.g. Hantro H1 on
NXP i.MX8MM) are available to WebRTC without requiring a runtime flag.

Note: The previous version of this patch also forced
IsGpuMemoryBufferNV12Supported() to return true and changed buffer
usage in the video frame pools. That was WRONG — it caused 30
failed GBM NV12 allocation attempts per second (Vivante GBM cannot
allocate NV12), leading to OOM within ~60 seconds. The NV12 check
does NOT gate encoder selection in Chromium 129; it only gates video
capture GPU memory buffer usage. The encoder works fine receiving
frames through SharedMemory (I420 -> MMAP copy path).

Tested on: NXP i.MX8MM EVK with Chromium 129.0.6668.100

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [i.MX specific]

---

--- a/media/base/media_switches.cc
+++ b/media/base/media_switches.cc
@@ -758,7 +758,7 @@

 BASE_FEATURE(kVaapiVideoEncodeLinux,
              "VaapiVideoEncoder",
-             base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+             base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);

 // Ignore the non-intel driver blacklist for VaapiVideoDecoder implementations.
 // Intended for manual usage only in order to gague the status of newer driver
